::ENG4_U06_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which opening is most focused for an oral presentation? {
~A. I will talk about transportation and maybe jobs.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
=B. Reliable transportation supports employment stability by reducing missed shifts.#Correct. This gives a clear and specific focus.
~C. Transportation is a thing people use.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. There are many topics to discuss.#Incorrect. This does not establish a focus.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: A city report links unreliable transit to increased worker absences. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Reliable transit causes worker absences.#Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
~B. A city report says absences never relate to transit.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
~C. Workers are absent because reports exist.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
=D. A city report connects unreliable transit with more worker absences.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which word best describes evidence from a trustworthy public report? {
=A. credible.#Correct. Credible means trustworthy or believable.
~B. decorative.#Incorrect. Decorative does not describe trustworthiness.
~C. random.#Incorrect. Random does not show reliability.
~D. unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated does not describe source quality.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence uses standard English? {
~A. The evidence credible because report.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~B. The evidence is credible it comes from a public report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
=C. The evidence is credible because it comes from a public report.#Correct. This is clear and complete.
~D. Because evidence credible public report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] What should come immediately after a focused claim in a short oral response? {
~A. An unrelated personal story.#Incorrect. This distracts from the focus.
~B. A conclusion with no support.#Incorrect. The listener needs evidence first.
=C. Credible evidence that supports the claim.#Correct. Evidence should support the focus.
~D. A new topic.#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: Clear emergency communication helps residents make informed safety decisions. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Clear emergency messages can help residents make informed safety choices.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~B. Emergency communication prevents all danger.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
~C. Residents never use emergency communication.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D. Safety decisions are unrelated to communication.#Incorrect. The source connects them.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which transition best introduces evidence in an oral response? {
~A. In conclusion,#Incorrect. This signals ending, not evidence.
~B. On a totally different topic,#Incorrect. This signals a shift away from the focus.
~C. Anyway,#Incorrect. This is informal and unfocused.
=D. According to the report,#Correct. This introduces source-based evidence.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A. The evidence thing is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The evidence supports the claim by showing a measurable pattern.#Correct. Supports, claim, and measurable are used precisely.
~C. The claim has stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. The report is report-like.#Incorrect. This is not precise.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which revision fixes the sentence? Draft: The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence. {
~A. The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~B. Focus clear helps listeners evidence.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=C. The focus is clear, and it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Correct. This correctly joins two complete ideas.
~D. Because the focus clear.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which response best explains evidence after presenting it? {
=A. This matters because missed shifts can reduce income and job stability.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~B. That is evidence.#Incorrect. This labels but does not explain.
~C. Moving to a new unrelated topic.#Incorrect. This does not explain evidence.
~D. Evidence is evidence.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which oral response problem is most serious? {
~A. The speaker uses one clear transition.#Incorrect. A transition helps.
~B. The speaker explains why evidence matters.#Incorrect. Explanation is useful.
~C. The speaker ends with a conclusion.#Incorrect. A conclusion can help.
=D. The speaker lists evidence but never states a focus.#Correct. Without focus, listeners cannot organize the evidence.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: The study found that tutoring increased course completion rates. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Tutoring lowered course completion rates.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=B. The study found that students completed courses more often when tutoring was available.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C. The study ignored tutoring.#Incorrect. Tutoring is the focus.
~D. Course completion was unrelated to support.#Incorrect. The source note connects them.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] What does concise mean in oral communication? {
~A. confusing and long.#Incorrect. Concise means brief and clear.
=B. brief, clear, and focused.#Correct. Concise speech avoids unnecessary wording.
~C. unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. Concise communication stays focused.
~D. informal and vague.#Incorrect. Concise does not mean informal.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is ready for a formal oral presentation? {
~A. The report says stuff about transit.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Transit stuff is in the report.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=C. The report explains how transit reliability affects worker attendance.#Correct. This is clear and formal.
~D. Report transit worker attendance affects.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which conclusion best reinforces a focused oral response? {
=A. Therefore, transportation access should be treated as a workforce stability issue.#Correct. This restates the significance of the focus.
~B. Anyway, there are many unrelated issues.#Incorrect. This weakens the conclusion.
~C. That is all I know.#Incorrect. This is not a strong conclusion.
~D. Evidence was mentioned earlier.#Incorrect. This does not reinforce the point.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: Recent survey data show that residents prefer clear text alerts during emergencies. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Residents dislike all emergency alerts.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~B. Emergencies do not require communication.#Incorrect. The note concerns emergency communication.
~C. Text alerts are illegal.#Incorrect. This is unsupported.
=D. Recent survey results indicate that residents favor clear text alerts in emergencies.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] What makes evidence credible in an oral response? {
~A. It sounds dramatic.#Incorrect. Drama is not credibility.
~B. It is unrelated to the focus.#Incorrect. Relevant evidence is needed.
=C. It comes from a trustworthy and relevant source.#Correct. Credibility depends on source quality and relevance.
~D. It is impossible to verify.#Incorrect. Credible evidence can be checked.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which term means the main point a speaker wants listeners to understand? {
=A. focus.#Correct. Focus is the central point.
~B. decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration is not the main point.
~C. fragment.#Incorrect. Fragment is a grammar term.
~D. distraction.#Incorrect. Distraction pulls away from the point.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Draft: Because the evidence is credible. Which revision is complete? {
~A. Because the evidence is credible.#Incorrect. This remains a fragment.
=B. Because the evidence is credible, listeners can trust the claim more easily.#Correct. This completes the dependent clause.
~C. Evidence credible because.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~D. The evidence because credible.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which speaking plan is most effective? {
~A. Evidence, evidence, unrelated story, no focus.#Incorrect. This lacks organization.
~B. Conclusion first, no evidence, new topic.#Incorrect. This weakens communication.
~C. Several unrelated claims.#Incorrect. This lacks focus.
=D. Focus, credible evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This is organized for listeners.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase mistake should a speaker avoid? {
=A. Changing the source meaning while restating it.#Correct. Accurate paraphrase must preserve meaning.
~B. Using new wording.#Incorrect. Paraphrase uses new wording.
~C. Keeping the central idea.#Incorrect. That is necessary.
~D. Mentioning the source briefly.#Incorrect. That can help credibility.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Why should a speaker explain evidence instead of only naming it? {
~A. Explanation replaces the need for evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
~B. Explanation should be unrelated.#Incorrect. It should be connected.
=C. Explanation helps listeners understand how the evidence supports the focus.#Correct. Explanation connects support to the point.
~D. Evidence is never important.#Incorrect. Evidence is important.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which word best describes information that directly supports the focus? {
~A. decorative.#Incorrect. Decorative does not mean supportive.
~B. random.#Incorrect. Random suggests no clear connection.
~C. contradictory.#Incorrect. Contradictory means opposing.
=D. relevant.#Correct. Relevant means connected to the matter at hand.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence avoids vague language? {
~A. The report has stuff.#Incorrect. Stuff is vague.
=B. The report includes attendance data from the last school year.#Correct. This is specific.
~C. The report is kind of about things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. The report does something.#Incorrect. This lacks precision.
}

::ENG4_U06_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] What is the best final check for a short oral response? {
=A. The response has a clear focus, credible evidence, explanation, and conclusion.#Correct. This confirms the full oral communication structure.
~B. The response includes several unrelated topics.#Incorrect. That weakens focus.
~C. The response avoids evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
~D. The response begins and ends with confusion.#Incorrect. Clarity is required.
}
